    <h1>Jacob Hope: A Name for the Round Lake Beach Baby &mdash; How Genetic Genealogy Reopened a 2001 Cold Case</h1>

    <div class="post__content">
      <div class="res" id="answer-box">
        <p class="res__k">The short version</p>
        <p>The newborn boy the community named <strong>&ldquo;Jacob Hope&rdquo;</strong> was found dead on <strong>August 26, 2001</strong>, in a bag in a dumpster behind a grocery store on Rollins Road in Round Lake Beach, Illinois. The case went cold for more than two decades. After Round Lake Beach police reopened it in early 2024 and submitted the child&rsquo;s DNA for genetic genealogy testing, investigators identified his biological parents.</p>
        <p>On <strong>July 14, 2026</strong>, the Round Lake Beach Police Department and the Lake County State&rsquo;s Attorney&rsquo;s Office announced that <strong>Jennifer Lebron, 48</strong>, the child&rsquo;s mother, was charged with <strong>two counts of involuntary manslaughter &mdash; not murder</strong>. She appeared in Lake County court on July 15, 2026, was released pending trial, and is scheduled to return to court on <strong>August 12, 2026</strong>.</p>
        <p><strong>Jennifer Lebron has been charged, not convicted.</strong> Under the law she is presumed innocent unless and until proven guilty in a court of law. The baby&rsquo;s father, identified through the same investigation, is <strong>not suspected of any wrongdoing</strong>.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="evidence evidence--accent">Case status: Charges filed July 14, 2026 &middot; Court-active &middot; Next hearing August 12, 2026 &middot; No conviction.</p>

      <h2 id="new-development">New development (July 2026): a charge in Jacob Hope&rsquo;s case</h2>
      <p>For nearly 25 years, the child had a name the community gave him but no name of his own on the record, and no one held to account. That changed in July 2026.</p>
      <p>On <strong>July 14, 2026</strong>, the Round Lake Beach Police Department and the Lake County State&rsquo;s Attorney&rsquo;s Office announced in a joint statement that <strong>Jennifer Lebron, 48, of Lake Villa</strong>, had been charged in connection with the infant&rsquo;s death. Authorities identify Lebron as the baby&rsquo;s mother.</p>
      <p>The precise charge matters, and we state it precisely: Lebron was charged with <strong>two counts of involuntary manslaughter</strong>. This is <strong>not</strong> a murder charge. In 2001, a <em>different</em> woman had been charged with first-degree murder in the case, but investigators later determined she had never been pregnant; those charges were dismissed in October 2001, and the case went cold.</p>
      <p>Lebron appeared before Lake County Judge Rhonda K. Bruno on <strong>July 15, 2026</strong>. Prosecutors did not seek to detain her; the judge imposed a curfew and barred her from using drugs or alcohol while awaiting trial, and she was released. She is scheduled to return to court on <strong>August 12, 2026</strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>A charge is an accusation, not a verdict.</strong> Jennifer Lebron is presumed innocent unless and until she is convicted in a court of law. We will update this file as the case proceeds. Whether Ms. Lebron has retained an attorney was not stated in available reporting.</p>
